@node Porting Strategy
@section Porting Strategy

@cindex transliteration
@cindex porting strategy
Phobos uses @strong{manual transliteration} (porting reference,
strategy 3).  Game-logic files (@file{p_*}, @file{g_game}, @file{info},
@file{tables}, @file{m_random}, @file{m_fixed}) keep their function
boundaries and call order, so a diff against @code{linuxdoom-1.10}
stays readable and the random-number call sequence is preserved.
Rendering (@file{r_*}), video (@file{v_video}), and the front end
(@file{st_*}, @file{hu_*}, @file{am_map}, @file{m_menu},
@file{wi_stuff}, @file{f_finale}) keep their algorithms but use
idiomatic data structures.  Platform code (@file{i_*}) is rewritten
from scratch.

@cindex doomgeneric
@cindex c2rust
The other two strategies were rejected:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A @code{doomgeneric} shim plays on day one but keeps C in the tree
until the end, which NG-7 forbids.
@item
@code{c2rust} output carries C's global-pointer shape into thousands of
@code{unsafe} blocks.  Goal G3 would then mean refactoring the whole
tree a second time.
@end itemize

@cindex Chocolate Doom
Kreimeier's pre-release cleanup of the source added a few bugs.
Chocolate Doom documents the corrections.  The port follows Chocolate
Doom's corrected behaviour and credits it in @file{CREDITS.md}
(§15.3, OQ-4).

@node Crate Layout
@section Crate Layout

@cindex crates
@example
phobos/
+-- crates/
|   +-- phobos-fixed/    Fixed (16.16), Angle (BAM), trig tables, P_Random
|   +-- phobos-wad/      WAD reader, IWAD identification, lump namespace
|   +-- phobos-data/     Map, texture, patch, flat, sprite, sound decoding
|   +-- phobos-sim/      Deterministic game simulation (forbid unsafe)
|   +-- phobos-game/     Game flow: modes, missions, demos, saves, cheats
|   +-- phobos-render/   Software renderer to an 8-bit framebuffer
|   +-- phobos-ui/       Status bar, HUD, automap, menus, intermission, finales
|   +-- phobos-audio/    Mixer, SFX, MUS sequencer, OPL backend
|   +-- phobos-gpu/      wgpu presenter (M3) and enhanced renderer (M8)
|   +-- phobos-app/      winit event loop, CLI, config, input mapping
|   +-- phobos-tools/    phobos-tools wad|demo|map inspection and CI checks
+-- fuzz/                cargo-fuzz targets
@end example

Dependency rules, enforced by @code{cargo deny} bans and checked at
review:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{phobos-sim} depends only on @code{phobos-fixed} and
@code{phobos-data}.
@item
No renderer depends on @code{phobos-wad}.  Renderers receive decoded
resources.
@item
Only @code{phobos-app} and @code{phobos-gpu} depend on windowing or GPU
crates.
@end itemize

@node State Ownership
@section State Ownership

@cindex global state
@cindex thinkers
The C engine keeps its state in mutable globals.  Phobos keeps it in
owned structs passed down the call tree:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{Level} owns the map geometry, sectors, lines, blockmap, and
reject table.
@item
@code{World} owns the @code{Level}, the thinker arena, the players, and
the random indices.  A tic is
@code{fn tick(&mut World, &[TicCmd; MAXPLAYERS])}.
@item
@strong{Thinkers} live in an arena addressed by generational handles.
An index-linked list inside the arena keeps the vanilla insertion and
iteration order.  That order decides the random-call sequence, so demo
sync depends on it.  Pointer fields such as @code{mobj_t.target} and
@code{tracer} become @code{Option<MobjHandle>}.  A handle to a removed
thing resolves to @code{None}.  In C, the same case reads freed memory
that the zone allocator had not yet reused.  The port reproduces that
observable result explicitly.
@item
Sector thing lists and blockmap links keep their vanilla link order,
for the same reason.
@item
The arithmetic overflows that vanilla relies on use @code{wrapping_*}
operations explicitly.  @code{overflow-checks = true} stays on in test
profiles so that any accidental overflow fails.
@end itemize

@node Concurrency Model
@section Concurrency Model

@cindex concurrency
@cindex Amdahl's law
@multitable @columnfractions .2 .28 .52
@headitem Work @tab Model @tab Decision and trade-off
@item Simulation tic
@tab Serial, one thread
@tab Determinism needs one exact order of random calls and thinker
updates.  A tic costs well under 1 ms, so by Amdahl's law parallelism
could save almost nothing and would risk desync.  Serial is chosen and
documented here.
@item Event loop and presentation
@tab winit main thread
@tab winit requires the main thread on several platforms.  Frames go
from the game thread to the presenter through a triple buffer, so
neither side waits for the other.
@item Software renderer
@tab Serial at 320x200.  Optional banded parallel drawing with Rayon at
2x and 4x scale (R-056)
@tab BSP traversal is serial by nature.  Column and span drawing for
screen bands can run in parallel once clipping is done.  Adopted only
if a benchmark shows a gain over serial at that scale.
@item Audio mixing and OPL synthesis
@tab cpal callback thread
@tab The simulation sends sound commands over a bounded lock-free SPSC
queue and never blocks.  If the queue is full, the oldest command is
dropped and counted.
@item Level load
@tab Rayon over textures
@tab Texture compositing and patch decoding are embarrassingly
parallel.  Kept only if it beats serial on the reference machine.
@item Demo corpus in CI
@tab One process per demo
@tab Shared-nothing parallelism over the corpus.
@end multitable

@node Rendering
@section Rendering

@cindex software renderer
@cindex wgpu
@table @asis
@item Software renderer (reference, M3)
A port of @file{r_*.c} that writes palette indices to a 320x200
framebuffer (or 2x / 4x scale, optional).  Colour maps, light
diminishing, fuzz, sky, masked mid-textures, and sprite clipping follow
vanilla.  Vanilla render limits (visplanes, drawsegs, vissprites) are
enforced by default and overflow into a logged, non-fatal fallback.
Vanilla crashes in this case; Phobos does not.  A limit-removing mode
is optional (R-085).

@item Presenter (M3)
The indexed frame is uploaded to a wgpu texture.  A small WGSL shader
expands it through @code{PLAYPAL} and applies palette flashes.  The
output is scaled to 4:3 aspect, with integer scaling as an option.  If
no GPU adapter is available, a @code{softbuffer} CPU path is used.

@item Enhanced renderer (M8)
A wgpu renderer reads the same @code{RenderSnapshot}.  It stores static
level geometry in GPU buffers, draws sprites with instancing, and
applies colour-map lighting in the shader.  Dynamic lights,
post-processing, and ray-traced effects are optional.  Its output is
checked against the software renderer on golden scenes.
@end table

@node Audio
@section Audio

@cindex audio
@cindex OPL
@table @asis
@item Sound effects
DMX @code{DS*} lumps are decoded and resampled to the output rate.
There are 8 channels, using vanilla's priority and channel-stealing
rules, distance attenuation, and stereo separation (@file{s_sound.c}).

@item Music
The @code{MUS} sequencer drives an OPL3 emulator voiced from the IWAD's
@code{GENMIDI} bank, the vanilla sound.  Candidate crate:
@code{nuked-opl3}, a pure-Rust port.  It is at 0.1.0 and needs the §24
audit before adoption.  SoundFont MIDI is optional.

@item Degraded mode
If no audio device is available, the game runs silent and logs one
warning.
@end table

@node Platform and Files
@section Platform and Files

@cindex XDG
@cindex privacy
@itemize @bullet
@item
Windowing and input use winit.  Gamepads use gilrs (optional).
@item
File locations follow XDG.  Configuration goes in
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/phobos/phobos.toml}; saves and demos in
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/phobos/}.
@item
IWAD search order: @code{-iwad}, @env{DOOMWADDIR}, @env{DOOMWADPATH},
the XDG data directories plus @file{doom/}, then the current directory.
@item
No network access in any build (§9).
@item
The engine uses no fonts of its own except on the no-IWAD screen and
the debug overlay.  Those use bundled Share Tech Mono / Inconsolata
(OFL, §12) and the @code{steelbore} theme (§11, §11.6).  In-game menus
and HUD use IWAD graphics.
@end itemize

